    "q-143452": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143452",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "The weights of sugar in different types of cookies are normally distributed with a mean of $15\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\text{g}.$ Suppose $16$ cookies are selected randomly. What is the probability that the mean amount of sugar per cookie for this sample will be more than $14.7\\,\\text{g},$ but less than $15.3\\,\\text{g}?$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> \n  <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th>\n  <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n  <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n  <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n  <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th>\n  <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2420\\,$ </td>\n  <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2389\\,$ </td>\n  <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2358\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n  <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th>\n    <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2743\\,$ </td>\n    <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2709\\,$ </td>\n    <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2676\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n  <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th>\n  <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3085\\,$ </td>\n  <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3050\\,$ </td>\n  <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3015\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n\n<th><p></p>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> \n  <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th>\n  <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n  <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n  <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n  <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.5\\,$</th>\n  <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6915\\,$ </td>\n  <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6950\\,$ </td>\n  <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6985\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n  <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.6\\,$</th>\n    <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7257\\,$ </td>\n    <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7291\\,$ </td>\n    <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7324\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n  <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th>\n  <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7580\\,$ </td>\n  <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7611\\,$ </td>\n  <td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7642\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the population mean $\\mu =15,$ the population standard deviation $\\sigma =2,$ and that the sample size $n=16.$ Therefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(15,\\frac{{2}^{2}}{16}) \\\\  & ∼N(15,0.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(14.7<\\overset{―}{X}<15.3).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(14.7<\\overset{―}{X}<15.3) & =P(\\frac{14.7-15}{\\sqrt{0.25}}<Z<\\frac{15.3-15}{\\sqrt{0.25}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.6<Z<0.6) \\\\  & =Φ(0.6)-Φ(-0.6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.6) & ≈0.7257 \\\\ Φ(-0.6) & ≈0.2743.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(14.7<\\overset{―}{X}<15.3) & =Φ(0.6)-Φ(-0.6) \\\\  & ≈0.7257-0.2743 \\\\  & =0.4514.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
